{
  "version": 1,
  "questionId": "q16",
  "speechText": "For me, I start with least privilege. The agent should only have the tools it really needs. That's because the model should not have direct access to internal services or databases. Then I would expose a small set of backend tools. The model can decide which tool it needs. But the backend still defines the tool contract, validates the arguments, and checks permission before doing it. For sensitive actions, I would also require user confirmation when needed. For tracking, I keep clear logs with the important IDs, status, errors, and performance metrics, such as latency, processing time, and memory usage. This helps me monitor the system, find what went wrong, and debug problems more easily. For safe logging, I avoid sensitive data. For write actions, I use idempotency so retries do not create duplicates. So overall, the model can decide which tool it needs. But the backend still defines the tool contract, validates the arguments, and checks permission before doing it.",
